// Original short lessons for shared topics, one per line:
// "id = explanation | point; point; point | practice task | question => answer".
// A lesson appears in every roadmap that uses the topic.
export const sharedLessons = `
internet = The internet is a network of networks. Data is cut into small packets, each carrying source and destination addresses, and routers pass them along one hop at a time until they arrive. | Packets can take different routes and arrive out of order; IP moves packets, while TCP puts them back in order; Your router connects your home network to your provider | Run traceroute (tracert on Windows) to a website and name three kinds of network you can see in the hops. | Why can one web page arrive over several routes? => Each packet is routed independently, and the receiver reassembles them.
http = HTTP is a request and response conversation. The client sends a method, a path and headers; the server answers with a status code, headers and usually a body. Each request stands alone, so state such as sign-in travels in cookies or tokens. | GET reads and should not change data; 2xx succeeded, 3xx moved, 4xx your request, 5xx the server; Headers describe content type, caching and identity | Open your browser's network panel, load a page and explain the method, status and content type of three requests. | What is the difference between 401 and 403? => 401 means you are not authenticated; 403 means you are known but not allowed.
dns = DNS turns a name such as example.com into an IP address. Your device asks a resolver, which follows the chain from root to top-level domain to the domain's own name servers, then caches the answer for its time to live. | A and AAAA records hold addresses; CNAME points one name at another; MX routes mail and TXT holds verification text | Use nslookup or dig to find the A, MX and TXT records of a domain you know. | Why can a DNS change take time to appear? => Resolvers keep cached answers until the time to live expires.
https-tls = TLS protects traffic from being read or changed on the way. The server proves its identity with a certificate signed by an authority, both sides agree on a shared key, and that fast symmetric key encrypts everything after. | Public-key cryptography starts the conversation, symmetric keys carry it; A certificate binds a name to a public key; HTTPS protects the path, not a dishonest site | Inspect a site's certificate and note who issued it, for which names and when it expires. | Does a padlock mean a site is trustworthy? => No. It means the connection is private and the name was verified, not that the owner is honest.
hosting = Hosting is a computer that is always reachable and answers requests for your site. A domain is the name people type, and DNS connects the two. | Static hosting serves files and is cheap and fast; Servers run your code on request; Deployment copies a tested version to the host | Publish a one-page site on a free static host and open it on your phone. | What connects a domain name to a server? => DNS records that point the name at the server or host.
html = HTML describes what content is, not how it looks. Choosing the right element gives you keyboard support, search visibility and screen reader meaning for free. | Use one h1 and ordered headings; Use button for actions and a for navigation; Every input needs a label | Rebuild an article page using only semantic elements, then read it with styles switched off. | Why is a clickable div worse than a button? => It has no keyboard focus, role or activation unless you rebuild all of them.
css = CSS selects elements and sets how they look. When rules conflict, the cascade decides by origin, specificity and order, so short simple selectors are easier to live with. | Every element is a box of content, padding, border and margin; Custom properties store reusable values; Prefer classes over ids and !important | Style a card, then change its whole theme by editing only custom properties. | Two rules target the same element with equal specificity. Which wins? => The one that appears later.
css-layout = Flexbox arranges items along one line and shares the spare space. Grid defines rows and columns together. Most pages use grid for the overall structure and flexbox inside components. | Use gap instead of margins between items; minmax and auto-fit make grids responsive; Keep the HTML order meaningful | Build a card gallery that moves from one to four columns without media queries. | When is grid the better choice? => When you need to align items in rows and columns at the same time.
responsive = Responsive design starts with a flexible layout that fits small screens, then adds changes only where the content needs them. | Use relative units and max-width; Set breakpoints where the design breaks, not for named devices; Container queries respond to available space | Take a desktop-only page and make it comfortable at 320 pixels wide with no sideways scroll. | Why start from the small screen? => It forces you to choose what matters, and enhancement is simpler than removal.
javascript = JavaScript makes pages react. Values are stored in variables, functions package behaviour, and objects and arrays hold structured data. Functions are values too, so you can pass them around. | Use const by default and let when a value changes; Use === to avoid surprising conversions; map, filter and reduce transform arrays | Write a function that takes a list of prices and returns the total with tax, then test edge cases. | What does const protect? => The variable cannot be reassigned, but an object it holds can still change.
dom = The browser turns HTML into a tree of objects called the DOM. JavaScript can find nodes, change them and listen for events, which travel down to the target and bubble back up. | querySelector finds elements; textContent is safer than innerHTML for text; One listener on a parent can serve many children | Build a list where items can be added and removed using a single click listener. | Why is innerHTML risky with user input? => It can run injected markup, which leads to cross-site scripting.
fetch-async = Network work takes time, so JavaScript continues and returns to it later. A promise represents the future result, and async/await lets you write that flow in reading order. | fetch only rejects on network failure, so check response.ok; Always design loading, error and empty states; Promise.all runs independent requests together | Load data from a public API and show a helpful message when it fails or returns nothing. | A server answers 500. Does fetch reject? => No. The promise resolves and response.ok is false.
typescript = TypeScript adds types that are checked before the code runs and then removed. Good types describe what is possible, so impossible states cannot be written. | Turn on strict mode; Prefer unions over many optional fields; Use unknown for outside data and check it | Model a request that is loading, failed or loaded so that data only exists when loaded. | Do types protect you from bad API data at run time? => No. They disappear, so outside data needs run-time validation.
npm = A package manager downloads libraries, records exact versions in a lock file and runs project scripts. Every dependency is code you trust, so add them deliberately. | package.json states what you want, the lock file what you got; Commit the lock file; Scripts document how to build and test | Create a project, add one dependency, write two scripts and explain the lock file. | Why commit the lock file? => Everyone, including the build server, installs the same versions.
git = Git stores snapshots called commits and lets you branch, compare and return. Working in small commits with clear messages gives you safety and a readable history. | The staging area chooses what enters a commit; Branches are cheap pointers to commits; revert is safer than rewriting shared history | Make a branch, create a conflict on purpose, resolve it and explain each step. | How do you undo a commit that others already have? => Use git revert, which adds a new commit that reverses it.
github = GitHub hosts repositories and adds collaboration: issues to discuss work, pull requests to propose it and checks to verify it. | A pull request should do one thing; Describe why, not only what; Protect the main branch | Open a pull request on your own repository with a clear description and a passing check. | What is the main benefit of a pull request? => Changes are discussed and verified before they reach the main branch.
code-review = Review shares knowledge and catches problems early. Reviewers look at design and behaviour first, and authors help by keeping changes small. | Explain the reason behind a comment; Separate must-fix from suggestions; Review the code, not the person | Review one open source pull request and write three specific, kind comments. | What most improves review quality? => Smaller changes, because reviewers can actually understand them.
unit-testing = A unit test checks one small behaviour quickly and without outside systems. Good tests describe what should happen, so they still pass after you improve the code inside. | Arrange, act, assert; Test behaviour, not private details; Include boundaries and failures | Write tests for a function that splits a bill, including zero people and uneven amounts. | A refactor breaks tests but not behaviour. What does that suggest? => The tests depend on implementation details.
integration-testing = Integration tests check that real parts work together, such as your code with a database. They are slower than unit tests but catch wiring mistakes. | Use a real database in a container; Start each test from a known state; Test errors at the boundary | Test an endpoint against a temporary database, including invalid input. | What do integration tests find that unit tests miss? => Mismatched assumptions between parts, such as queries, schemas and formats.
e2e-testing = End-to-end tests drive the product like a person. They give the most realistic confidence and cost the most to run, so cover only the most important journeys. | Find elements by role and label; Wait for outcomes, not fixed times; Keep each test independent | Automate sign-in and one key action, then make it pass ten times in a row. | Why avoid fixed waits? => They are slow when too long and flaky when too short.
a11y = Accessibility means people can use your product whatever their abilities or devices. Semantic HTML covers most of it; the rest is focus, contrast, text alternatives and testing. | Everything must work with a keyboard; Text contrast should reach 4.5 to 1; Use ARIA only when HTML cannot express it | Use one of your pages with only a keyboard, then with a screen reader, and fix what blocks you. | What is the first rule of ARIA? => Use a native element if one exists.
web-performance = Speed is what people feel: when content appears, when they can interact and whether the layout jumps. Measure first, because guesses are often wrong. | Largest images usually matter most; Send less JavaScript; Reserve space to avoid layout shifts | Measure a page with Lighthouse, fix the largest problem and measure again. | Why measure before optimising? => The real bottleneck is often not where you expect.
web-security = Browsers trust code from your site, so attackers try to make their code look like yours. Escape output, verify requests and limit what scripts can do. | XSS injects scripts, so encode output; CSRF abuses a signed-in browser, so check origin or tokens; A content security policy limits damage | Solve one cross-site scripting lab and explain the fix. | Does input validation alone stop XSS? => No. Output must be encoded for the place where it is used.
rest = REST models a system as resources with addresses. Standard methods and status codes make an API predictable, so clients can guess correctly. | Use nouns in paths and methods for actions; Return the right status code; Paginate every list | Design paths, methods and status codes for a library system before writing code. | Which methods should be safe to repeat? => GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent; POST usually is not.
graphql = GraphQL lets a client ask one endpoint for exactly the fields it needs, checked against a typed schema. It reduces over-fetching but makes caching and cost control your job. | The schema is the contract; Resolvers fetch each field; Batch to avoid repeated queries | Query a public GraphQL API for three nested fields and rewrite it with variables. | What is the N+1 problem? => One query for a list triggers another query for every item.
websockets = Normal HTTP waits for the client to ask. WebSockets keep a connection open so both sides can send at any time; server-sent events are simpler when only the server sends. | Polling is fine for slow updates; Reconnect and resume after drops; Authenticate the connection | Build a tiny live counter shared between two browser windows. | When are server-sent events enough? => When updates flow only from server to client.
authn = Authentication proves who someone is. Use proven libraries or providers, store only slow salted password hashes and offer a second factor. | Give the same message for unknown user and wrong password; Limit attempts; Protect sessions with secure, HTTP-only cookies | Draw the full sign-up, sign-in and password reset flow and mark where an attacker could interfere. | Why hide whether an email exists? => It stops attackers from collecting valid accounts.
authz = Authorisation decides what a known person may do. It must be checked on the server for every request, on the specific record requested. | Deny by default; Check ownership, not only role; Never trust hidden buttons or client checks | Try to read another user's record by changing an id in your own app, then fix it. | A user changes an id in the address and sees another account. What is missing? => An ownership check on the server.
oauth = OAuth lets an app act with limited permission without seeing a password. OpenID Connect adds identity on top, so the app also learns who signed in. | Use the authorisation code flow with PKCE; Request the smallest scopes; Verify state and token audience | Draw each redirect of "Sign in with" and label what is sent. | Is OAuth by itself a sign-in protocol? => No. It grants access; OpenID Connect adds identity.
jwt = A session stores state on the server and gives the browser an id. A JWT carries signed claims so the server need not look them up, but it is hard to cancel before it expires. | A JWT is signed, not hidden; Keep access tokens short-lived; Prefer HTTP-only cookies in browsers | Decode a JWT, list its claims and explain what would happen if it leaked. | Can you read a signed JWT without the key? => Yes. The signature prevents changes, not reading.
big-o = Big-O describes how work grows as input grows, ignoring constants. It helps you see which solution will still work at a million items. | Constant, logarithmic, linear, n log n, quadratic; Nested loops often mean quadratic; Memory has a cost too | Estimate and then measure two ways to find duplicates in a list. | Why can a quadratic algorithm be fine? => For small inputs it may be simple and fast enough.
data-structures = A data structure is a trade-off about which operations are fast. Arrays give fast access, hash maps fast lookup, trees order, and graphs connections. | Pick by the operation you do most; Hash lookup is fast on average; Trees stay fast only when balanced | Implement a stack and a hash map, then list the cost of each operation. | You check membership constantly. Which structure fits? => A hash set.
algorithms = An algorithm is a reliable method for a kind of problem. Recognising the pattern, such as divide and conquer or dynamic programming, matters more than memorising code. | Binary search needs sorted data; Recursion needs a base case; Dynamic programming reuses answers to subproblems | Solve one problem by brute force, then improve it and explain the gain. | What makes a problem suit dynamic programming? => Overlapping subproblems and answers built from smaller answers.
os-basics = The operating system shares the machine. It runs processes, gives each its own memory, schedules the CPU and controls files and devices. | Threads share memory, processes do not; Virtual memory isolates programs; System calls ask the kernel for work | Watch processes while you start a program and explain CPU, memory and threads. | Why can one crashing program leave others alone? => Each process has its own protected memory.
concurrency = Concurrency is making progress on several tasks. Trouble starts when tasks share data and their order is not fixed. | A race condition depends on timing; Locks protect but can deadlock; Passing messages avoids shared state | Write a counter that breaks with two threads, then make it correct. | How do you prevent deadlock with two locks? => Always take them in the same order.
networking = Networks are built in layers: links move frames, IP moves packets between networks, TCP or UDP moves data between programs, and protocols such as HTTP give it meaning. | An IP address names a host, a port names a program; A subnet mask splits network from host; Routers join networks | Find your address, mask, gateway and DNS server, and explain each. | Two devices cannot talk. What do you check first? => Link and address, then gateway, then DNS, working up the layers.
tcp-udp = TCP sets up a connection and guarantees order and delivery. UDP sends datagrams with no promises, which is faster when a late packet is useless. | TCP begins with a three-way handshake; UDP suits voice, games and DNS; QUIC builds reliability on UDP | Capture a TCP handshake and label the three packets. | Why does live voice prefer UDP? => Waiting for a lost packet causes a worse gap than skipping it.
sql = SQL describes the result you want and lets the database work out how. Most questions combine filtering, joining, grouping and sorting. | WHERE filters rows, HAVING filters groups; A LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched rows; NULL is unknown, not zero | Write five questions about a dataset and answer each with one query. | Why does WHERE x = NULL find nothing? => NULL is unknown, so use IS NULL.
db-design = Good design stores each fact once. Give every table a key, link tables with foreign keys and normalise until updates cannot contradict each other. | One-to-many uses a foreign key; Many-to-many needs a join table; Constraints protect data even from bugs | Model a clinic with patients, doctors and appointments, then list the rules the database enforces. | What problem does repeating a customer's address on every order cause? => Copies can disagree when one is updated.
db-indexes = An index is a sorted structure that lets the database find rows without reading the whole table. It speeds reads and slows writes a little. | Index columns used to filter and join; Column order matters in a composite index; Check with EXPLAIN | Time a slow query, add an index and compare the plans. | Why not index every column? => Each index costs storage and slows every write.
transactions = A transaction groups changes so that all happen or none do. Isolation decides what simultaneous transactions can see of each other. | Atomic, consistent, isolated, durable; Keep transactions short; Retry on serialisation failures | Simulate two people booking the last seat and prevent the double booking. | Money leaves one account and the app crashes. What saves you? => The transaction rolls back because it never committed.
postgres = PostgreSQL is a dependable open source relational database with rich types, strong constraints and excellent documentation. | Use roles with least privilege; Use timestamptz for moments in time; Back up and practise restoring | Install it, create two related tables and load sample data. | Which type stores a moment in time safely? => timestamptz.
nosql = NoSQL stores trade some relational features for a shape that fits a need: documents, key-value pairs, wide columns or graphs. | Model around how you read; Documents suit nested data; Many NoSQL stores relax consistency | Model a blog in documents and in tables, and compare three queries. | When is a relational database the safer default? => When data is related and rules must always hold.
redis = Redis keeps data in memory, so it is very fast. It is used for caching, counters, sessions, queues and rate limits, usually beside a main database. | Set an expiry on cached data; Choose the right structure, such as a sorted set for ranking; Plan for data loss | Cache a slow call for a minute and measure the difference. | What is the hard part of caching? => Knowing when cached data is no longer valid.
orm = An ORM maps rows to objects and writes common queries. It saves time, but you still need SQL to understand what it does. | Migrations version the schema; Watch for N+1 queries; Use raw SQL when it is clearer | Log the queries of one page and remove an N+1 problem. | What is a migration? => A versioned, repeatable change to the database schema.
statistics = Statistics helps you describe data and draw careful conclusions from samples. The key idea is uncertainty: another sample would give a slightly different answer. | Median resists outliers, mean does not; Standard deviation measures spread; A confidence interval expresses uncertainty | Compare the mean and median of real salaries and explain the gap. | What does a 95% confidence interval mean? => If you repeated the sampling many times, about 95% of such intervals would contain the true value.
probability = Probability measures how likely events are. Conditional probability updates that belief when you learn something, which is what Bayes' rule formalises. | Independent events multiply; Base rates matter; Expected value is a long-run average | Work out how likely illness is after a positive test for a rare disease. | Why can most positive tests for a rare disease be false? => Healthy people are so numerous that even a small error rate produces many false positives.
linear-algebra = Vectors describe points and directions, and matrices describe transformations. Data and models are stored as matrices, so most of machine learning is linear algebra. | A dot product measures alignment; Matrix multiplication composes transformations; Shapes must agree | Multiply two matrices by hand, then check with NumPy. | When can you multiply an m by n and a p by q matrix? => When n equals p.
calculus = A derivative tells how fast something changes. A gradient points uphill in many dimensions, so training a model steps the opposite way to reduce error. | The chain rule links nested functions; A zero gradient can be a minimum; Learning rate sets step size | Implement gradient descent to minimise a simple curve and plot each step. | What happens if the learning rate is too large? => Steps overshoot and the loss may grow.
python = Python reads almost like plain language and has libraries for nearly everything, which makes it a strong first language and a practical tool at work. | Indentation defines blocks; Lists, dictionaries and sets cover most needs; Use a virtual environment per project | Write a script that renames files safely, with a preview before changes. | Why use a virtual environment? => Each project keeps its own package versions.
numpy = NumPy stores numbers in compact arrays and applies operations to whole arrays at once, which is far faster than Python loops. | Vectorise instead of looping; Broadcasting stretches shapes; Slices are often views, not copies | Replace a loop with array operations and time both. | You change a slice and the original changes. Why? => The slice is a view of the same memory.
pandas = pandas works with labelled tables. You load, select, clean, group and join, usually in a readable chain. | loc selects by label, iloc by position; groupby splits, applies and combines; Check types and missing values first | Answer five questions about a public CSV with grouped summaries. | What is the first thing to check after loading data? => Shape, types and missing values.
data-cleaning = Real data has gaps, duplicates and mistakes. Cleaning means making documented decisions, because each one can change the conclusion. | Keep the raw data unchanged; Ask why a value is missing; Write each step in code | Clean a messy dataset and list every decision with a reason. | Why not simply drop rows with missing values? => Missingness may have a cause, so dropping can bias results.
data-viz = A chart answers one question. Choose the form that makes the comparison easy and remove whatever does not help. | Bars for comparison, lines for time, scatter for relationships; Start bars at zero; Do not rely on colour alone | Redraw a confusing chart so its point is clear in five seconds. | Why start a bar chart at zero? => Length encodes value, so a cut axis exaggerates differences.
spreadsheets = Spreadsheets are the quickest way to explore small data and share results. Structure and checks keep them reliable. | One row per record, one column per field; Use pivot tables to summarise; Avoid numbers typed inside formulas | Build a monthly budget with a pivot summary and a check total. | Why keep inputs in their own cells? => They are visible, changeable and used consistently.
eda = Exploratory analysis is getting to know data before modelling it: shapes, spread, relationships and oddities. | Plot distributions first; Look for outliers and impossible values; Write questions as you go | Explore a dataset and report three findings and two doubts. | Why explore before modelling? => Problems and patterns found early change which model makes sense.
dashboards = A dashboard supports a repeated decision. It should show current state, comparison and where to look next, and nothing else. | Design for one audience; Show context such as target or last month; Put the main number first | Sketch a dashboard for one person and cut half of it. | How do you know a dashboard is useful? => Someone makes a decision with it.
ab-testing = An experiment assigns people at random so that groups differ only by the change. Decide the measure, size and stopping rule before you start. | Randomise the right unit; Do not stop when it looks good; Check harm as well as gain | Plan a test for a sign-up button, including sample size and one risk. | Why is peeking a problem? => Repeated checks raise the chance of a false positive.
ml-basics = Machine learning finds patterns in examples and applies them to new cases. The goal is performance on unseen data, not a perfect score on the training set. | Supervised learning uses labels; Split train, validation and test; Overfitting memorises noise | Train a simple model, then make it overfit on purpose and show the gap. | Training accuracy is 99% and test 70%. What happened? => The model overfitted.
sklearn = scikit-learn gives many models one interface: fit, predict and score. Pipelines keep preprocessing and model together so nothing leaks from the test data. | Always build a baseline; Put scaling inside the pipeline; Set a random seed | Build a pipeline with preprocessing, a model and cross-validation. | Why fit the scaler only on training data? => Using test data leaks information and inflates results.
feature-eng = Features are the inputs a model sees. Good features express what you know about the problem in a form a model can use. | Encode categories suitably; Use only what is known at prediction time; Simple features often win | Add three features to a baseline and measure each one's effect. | What is target leakage? => A feature contains information that would not be available when predicting.
model-eval = Evaluation must match the cost of mistakes. Accuracy can look high while the model fails the cases that matter. | Use precision when false alarms cost more; Use recall when misses cost more; Always compare with a baseline | Evaluate a model on imbalanced data with three metrics and recommend one. | Why is accuracy misleading for rare events? => Predicting "never" can score very high.
neural-nets = A neural network stacks simple layers that multiply, add and apply a non-linear function. Backpropagation computes how each weight affects the error so it can be adjusted. | Without non-linearity, layers collapse into one; Loss measures error; Batches make training practical | Build a two-layer network with NumPy on a toy problem. | Why are activation functions needed? => They let the network model non-linear relationships.
pytorch = PyTorch provides tensors, automatic gradients and building blocks for models. Training is a loop you write: predict, measure loss, compute gradients, update. | Clear gradients each step; Switch between train and eval modes; Move model and data to the same device | Write a full training loop and plot loss for training and validation. | What happens if you forget to clear gradients? => They accumulate from earlier steps.
nlp = Natural language processing turns text into numbers a model can use, then into answers such as labels, entities or replies. | Tokens are pieces of text; Embeddings place similar meanings nearby; Start with a simple baseline | Classify short reviews with a simple model and study ten mistakes. | Why study errors by hand? => They show whether the data, labels or model is the problem.
transformers = A transformer lets every token look at the others through attention and decide what matters. This handles long context and trains well in parallel. | Attention weighs relevance; Position information must be added; Cost grows quickly with length | Trace attention by hand for a four-word sentence. | Why do transformers need position information? => Attention alone does not know word order.
llm-basics = A language model predicts the next token again and again. It learned patterns from text, so it can be fluent and wrong at the same time. | Context window limits what it can see; Temperature changes variety; It has no built-in knowledge of today | Ask the same factual question ten times and record how answers vary. | Why can a model invent a source? => It produces likely text, not verified facts.
prompting = A good prompt states the goal, the audience, the context and the format, and shows an example when style matters. Improve it by testing, not by guessing. | Be specific about what good looks like; Separate instructions from data; Ask for a format you can check | Improve one prompt over five versions and log what changed. | What usually helps more than clever wording? => Clear context and an example of the result.
llm-apis = An API call sends messages and settings and returns text or structured data. Real use needs timeouts, retries, limits and a plan for wrong answers. | Keep keys on the server; Stream long replies; Track tokens and cost | Call a model with a timeout, one retry and a cost log. | Where must the API key live? => On the server, never in the browser or repository.
embeddings = An embedding is a list of numbers that places text by meaning, so related passages are close even with different words. | Compare with cosine similarity; Use the same model for storing and searching; Similar is not the same as correct | Search twenty sentences by meaning and find one failure. | Why can keyword search still win? => Exact names and codes matter, and embeddings can blur them.
vector-db = A vector database finds the nearest embeddings quickly, often with filters such as user or date. It uses approximate search to stay fast. | Store text and metadata with each vector; Filter by permission; Measure recall against exact search | Store fifty passages with pgvector and query with a filter. | What does approximate search trade? => A little accuracy for a lot of speed.
rag = Retrieval-augmented generation finds relevant passages first, then asks the model to answer only from them. Most failures come from retrieval, so test that separately. | Chunk by meaning; Show citations; Say "not found" when evidence is missing | Build question answering over five documents and test ten questions. | The answer is wrong but the passage was never retrieved. What do you fix? => Retrieval: chunking, search or filters.
agents = An agent lets a model choose steps and tools in a loop until a goal is met. Start with a fixed workflow and add freedom only where it helps. | Limit steps, time and cost; Log every action; Ask a person before consequences | Build a loop with one tool and a limit of five steps. | When is a workflow better than an agent? => When the steps are known in advance.
tool-use = Tool calling lets a model request a function with arguments. Your code decides whether to run it, validates the input and returns the result. | Describe tools clearly; Validate every argument; Return helpful errors | Give a model a calculator and a weather lookup, and handle a bad argument. | Who executes the tool? => Your code, after checking the request.
mcp = The Model Context Protocol is a common way to offer tools and data to AI applications, so one integration can serve many clients. | Servers expose tools, resources and prompts; Clients decide what to allow; Treat returned text as untrusted | Connect one read-only server and list what it can access. | What risk does a connected server add? => Its output may contain instructions, and its tools may have more access than needed.
llm-evals = Evaluation turns "seems good" into evidence. Collect real cases, define what correct means, score automatically where possible and rerun after every change. | Start with twenty cases; Include hard and harmful inputs; Compare with the previous version | Write a twenty-case test set and a simple grader for one prompt. | Why keep a fixed test set? => It shows whether a change improved or broke behaviour.
ai-safety = AI systems fail in new ways: they can be tricked by text, reveal private data or treat groups unfairly. Design assuming it will sometimes be wrong. | Untrusted text is data, not instructions; Give least privilege; Keep people in control of consequences | Try to make your own assistant ignore its rules, then reduce what success could do. | What limits the damage of prompt injection? => Few permissions and human approval for risky actions.
fine-tuning = Fine-tuning continues training on your examples to change style or skill. It does not reliably add facts, and it needs good data and evaluation. | Try prompting and retrieval first; Hold out test data; A few hundred excellent examples beat many poor ones | Prepare fifty examples and define how you would prove improvement. | Which need suits fine-tuning? => A consistent style or format that prompting cannot hold.
linux = Linux runs most servers. Files, permissions, processes and services are the four ideas that explain almost everything you do there. | Everything has an owner and permissions; Services run in the background; Logs tell you what happened | Create a user, a service and a log entry on a test machine. | What does permission 640 allow? => Owner read and write, group read, others nothing.
shell = The shell joins small programs into useful ones. Scripts make repeated work reliable when they quote variables and stop on errors. | Pipe output into the next command; Quote variables; Use set -euo pipefail carefully | Write a backup script that checks its inputs and reports failure. | Why quote a variable such as "$file"? => Spaces or wildcards would otherwise split or expand it.
ssh = SSH gives encrypted access to another machine. Key pairs are safer than passwords: the private key stays with you, the public key goes to the server. | Protect keys with a passphrase; Disable password sign-in; Tunnels forward ports safely | Create a key, connect to a machine and disable passwords there. | Which key do you copy to the server? => The public key.
docker = A container packages a program with what it needs and runs it as an isolated process. Images are built in layers, so order affects build speed. | Copy dependency files first to use the cache; Run as a non-root user; Volumes keep data | Containerise a small app and reduce the image size by half. | Where does data go when a container is removed? => It is lost unless stored in a volume.
compose = Compose describes several services in one file so a whole system starts with one command. | Services reach each other by name; Health checks order startup; Keep secrets out of the file | Run an app, a database and a cache together with seed data. | How does the app find the database? => By its service name on the shared network.
kubernetes = Kubernetes keeps the state you declare. You ask for three copies and it starts, replaces and moves them to keep three running. | Pods are replaceable; Services give a stable address; Set requests and limits | Deploy an app to a local cluster, delete a pod and watch it return. | A pod dies. What brings it back? => Its controller, such as a Deployment.
ci-cd = Continuous integration builds and tests every change. Continuous delivery keeps the result ready to release, so releases are small and boring. | Fail fast on the cheapest checks; Build once, deploy the same artefact; Make rollback easy | Create a pipeline that blocks a merge when a test fails. | Why build the artefact only once? => So what you tested is exactly what you release.
gh-actions = GitHub Actions runs workflows when something happens in a repository. A workflow has jobs, and each job has steps on a fresh machine. | Cache dependencies; Give the token the least permission; Pin third-party actions | Write a workflow that lints and tests every pull request. | Why pin an action to a commit? => A moved tag could run different code.
iac = Infrastructure as code describes servers and services in files, so changes are reviewed, repeatable and recoverable. | Preview before applying; Keep state safe and shared; Never edit by hand afterwards | Create and destroy a small resource from code twice. | What is drift? => The real infrastructure no longer matches the code.
ansible = Configuration management brings machines to a described state. Good tasks are idempotent: running again changes nothing. | Describe state, not steps; Group hosts in an inventory; Encrypt secrets | Write a playbook that installs and configures a web server, then run it twice. | How do you recognise an idempotent playbook? => The second run reports no changes.
cloud-basics = Cloud means renting computing by use. The provider protects the platform, and you protect your data, access and configuration. | Regions contain isolated zones; IaaS, PaaS and SaaS differ in what you manage; Costs follow usage | List who is responsible for five tasks on a virtual machine and on a managed database. | Who secures access to your storage bucket? => You do.
iam = Identity and access management decides who can do what to which resource. Most cloud incidents start with permissions that were too wide. | Grant the least needed; Prefer roles to long-lived keys; Review and remove unused access | Write a policy that can read one bucket and nothing else. | Why prefer roles to access keys? => They provide short-lived credentials that need no storage.
compute = Cloud compute ranges from virtual machines you manage to functions you only write. More control means more work. | Match size to measured load; Autoscaling follows demand; Managed options reduce patching | Compare running the same small app on a server, a container and a function. | When does a virtual machine make sense? => When you need control of the system or unusual software.
object-storage = Object storage keeps files under keys, cheaply and durably. It is ideal for images, backups and data, but it is not a disk. | Block public access by default; Version important data; Lifecycle rules cut cost | Store a file, share it with a time-limited link and add a lifecycle rule. | How do you share one private file safely? => With a signed link that expires.
vpc = A virtual network is your private space in the cloud. Public subnets reach the internet; private ones hold databases and internal services. | Route tables decide paths; Security groups filter by service; Keep data private | Draw a network with a public load balancer and a private database. | Why put a database in a private subnet? => It cannot be reached directly from the internet.
serverless = Serverless runs code when an event arrives and charges for use. You avoid managing servers and accept limits such as cold starts. | Keep functions small and stateless; Make handlers idempotent; Set timeouts | Write a function that resizes an uploaded image. | Why must handlers be idempotent? => Events can be delivered more than once.
cloud-cost = Cloud bills grow quietly. Visibility, ownership and turning things off matter more than clever discounts. | Tag by owner; Set budgets and alerts; Right-size from measurements | Set a budget alert and find one unused resource. | What is usually the quickest saving? => Removing or scaling down what nobody uses.
monitoring = Monitoring tells you whether users are being served. Alert on symptoms people feel, and make each alert something a person can act on. | Watch latency, traffic, errors and saturation; Percentiles beat averages; Every alert needs a response | Add two alerts to an app and write what to do for each. | Why alert on symptoms, not every cause? => Causes are endless; symptoms show real impact.
logging = Logs record events so you can answer questions later. Structured fields make them searchable; private data makes them dangerous. | Use levels consistently; Add a request id; Never log secrets | Add structured logs to one request path and find one request by id. | What should never appear in logs? => Passwords, tokens and unnecessary personal data.
tracing = A trace follows one request across services as timed spans, showing where time and errors really occur. | Pass the trace context along; Sample wisely; Link traces to logs | Trace one request through two services and find the slowest span. | What does a trace show that logs do not? => The full path and timing across services.
nginx = A reverse proxy stands in front of your app. It ends TLS, serves files, balances load and shields the app from direct traffic. | Forward the original host and address; Set timeouts and size limits; Compress text | Put a proxy with HTTPS in front of a local app. | Why does the app see the proxy's address? => The proxy makes the connection, so the original must be passed in a header.
load-balancing = A load balancer spreads requests over several servers and stops sending traffic to unhealthy ones. | Health checks must test real ability; Keep servers stateless; Balance the balancer | Run two copies of an app behind a balancer and stop one. | What makes adding servers easy? => Keeping session state outside them.
caching = Caching keeps a copy closer to where it is needed. It can exist in the browser, the CDN, the app and the database. | Set clear lifetimes; Include everything that changes the result in the key; Plan invalidation | Add a cache to one read and prove it is never wrongly shared. | What happens if a cache key omits the user? => One person may receive another's data.
cdn = A CDN keeps copies of files in many places, so users download from nearby and your server does less. | Use versioned file names; Cache static files for a long time; Be careful with private pages | Compare load times with and without a CDN. | How do you update a file cached for a year? => Change its name.
secrets = A secret is anything that grants access. Keep it out of code and history, give it to as few as possible and be ready to replace it. | Use a secrets manager; Rotate regularly; A leaked secret must be revoked, not hidden | Scan a repository for secrets and write a rotation plan. | You committed a key and deleted it. Is it safe? => No. It remains in history, so revoke it.
gitops = In GitOps the repository states what should run, and an agent makes the cluster match. Changing production means merging a reviewed change. | Declarative and versioned; The agent pulls changes; Reverting is a rollback | Deploy by commit and roll back with a revert. | What fixes a manual change in the cluster? => The agent restores the declared state.
slo = A service level objective states how reliable a service should be for users. The gap to 100% is an error budget you may spend on change. | Measure what users feel; 100% is the wrong goal; Budget exhausted means slow down | Write one objective for a service and calculate its monthly budget. | What does 99.9% allow in a 30-day month? => About 43 minutes of failure.
incidents = In an incident, first reduce harm, then find the cause. Clear roles and calm communication matter more than heroics. | Name a coordinator; Roll back early; Review without blame | Run a practice incident and write a short review with two actions. | Why blameless reviews? => People share what really happened, so the system can improve.
cia = Security protects confidentiality, integrity and availability. Controls work in layers, so one failure is not a disaster. | Grant least privilege; Assume a layer will fail; Balance risk with cost | Name one threat to each of the three for a school system. | Ransomware mainly attacks which property? => Availability, and often confidentiality too.
threat-modeling = Threat modelling asks four questions: what are we building, what can go wrong, what will we do, and did it work? | Draw data flows and trust boundaries; STRIDE prompts ideas; Rank by likelihood and impact | Model one feature in thirty minutes and choose two mitigations. | When is the best time to do it? => During design, before building.
owasp-top10 = The OWASP Top 10 summarises the most serious web risks. Broken access control leads the list because it is common and easy to miss. | Check access on the server; Use parameters in queries; Keep components updated | Map three risks to your own project and note the protection. | What stops SQL injection? => Parameterised queries.
cryptography = Hashing fingerprints data, symmetric encryption uses one shared key, and public-key cryptography uses a pair. Use vetted libraries and never invent your own. | Hashing is one-way; Encryption needs key management; Signatures prove origin and integrity | Hash, encrypt and sign a message with a library and explain each. | Which proves a message was not changed and who sent it? => A digital signature.
password-storage = Passwords are stored as slow, salted hashes so that a stolen database is expensive to crack. | Use Argon2id, scrypt or bcrypt; A salt makes equal passwords differ; Fast hashes are unsuitable | Hash one password twice and explain why the results differ. | Why not encrypt passwords? => Whoever gets the key can read them all.
supply-chain = Most code you ship was written by others. Know it, update it and verify it, because attackers target dependencies too. | Keep few dependencies; Automate updates and alerts; Lock and review versions | List your direct dependencies and remove one. | Why does a lock file help security? => It fixes exact versions and their checksums.
clean-code = Clean code is easy for the next person to read and change. Clear names and small functions do most of the work. | Name by intent; Remove duplication when it is the same idea; Improve as you pass | Refactor one long function in small tested steps. | When is duplication acceptable? => When two pieces look alike but change for different reasons.
oop = Objects keep data with the behaviour that protects it. Prefer combining small objects to deep inheritance. | Hide internal state; Depend on behaviour, not concrete classes; Composition is more flexible | Model a parking garage without inheritance, then discuss where it would help. | Why can deep inheritance hurt? => A change in a parent affects every child in hidden ways.
solid = SOLID is five rules of thumb: one reason to change, extend without editing, honest subtypes, small interfaces, and depending on abstractions. | Use them when change hurts; Over-applying adds layers; Tests reveal tight coupling | Find one class with two reasons to change and split it. | What does dependency inversion make easier? => Replacing details, for example with a test double.
design-patterns = A pattern names a solution that appears again and again, with its trade-offs. The name helps teams talk; the problem should come first. | Strategy swaps behaviour; Observer spreads events; Adapter fits interfaces | Find a pattern in code you use and explain why it is there. | When should you not use a pattern? => When simpler code solves the problem.
ddd = Domain-driven design puts business language in the code and draws boundaries where meanings change. | Use the words experts use; A bounded context owns its model; Aggregates protect rules | Interview someone about their work and list ten terms with meanings. | Why can "customer" need two models? => Sales and support mean different things by it.
monolith = A monolith is one deployable application. With clear modules it is simple to build, test and run, so it is the right start for most teams. | Keep module boundaries strict; Share through interfaces; Split only for a proven reason | Draw the modules of an app and the allowed dependencies. | What is a distributed monolith? => Separate services that must still be changed and deployed together.
microservices = Microservices let teams release independently by owning separate services and data. The price is networks, operations and consistency. | One service owns its data; Expect partial failure; Automate delivery first | Split one feature on paper and list the new failure modes. | What should two services never share? => A database.
event-driven = Services publish facts such as "order placed", and others react. This reduces coupling but makes flow harder to see. | Events are past tense facts; Consumers must handle repeats; Trace across services | Design the events of an order and who listens. | Why must consumers be idempotent? => The same event may arrive more than once.
queues = A queue holds work until a consumer is ready, smoothing bursts and isolating failures. Streams keep an ordered log that many can replay. | Acknowledge after the work is done; Use a dead-letter queue; Order is limited to a partition | Move email sending to a queue with retries. | Why acknowledge after processing? => If the worker crashes, the message is delivered again.
cap = When a network splits, a distributed system must choose between answering with possibly stale data and refusing to answer. | Partitions will happen; Choose per operation; Otherwise you trade latency and consistency | Choose behaviour for a bank balance and a like counter. | Which would you favour for a like counter? => Availability, because slight staleness is harmless.
scalability = Scaling means serving more without failing. Find the bottleneck, then scale that part up, out or away with caching. | Stateless services scale out; Databases are usually the limit; Measure under load | Load test an endpoint and report the first bottleneck. | Why is scaling a database harder than an app server? => It holds state that must stay consistent.
system-design = System design turns needs into parts and explains the trade-offs. There is rarely one answer, so the reasoning matters most. | Clarify needs and numbers first; Start simple; Name what could fail | Design a link shortener in thirty minutes and list three trade-offs. | What do you ask before drawing boxes? => What it must do, for how many, and how reliable and fast it must be.
adr = An architecture decision record is a short note of the context, the choice and its consequences. It stops the same argument returning. | One decision per record; Record rejected options; Never rewrite, supersede | Write a one-page record for a real choice. | Why keep a decision that was later reversed? => It explains the history and the reasons.
twelve-factor = Twelve-factor apps are easy to deploy anywhere: configuration comes from the environment, processes are stateless and logs are streams. | Keep config out of code; Treat services as attached resources; Keep environments alike | Check an app against the list and fix two gaps. | Where does a database address belong? => In the environment, not the code.
agile = Agile work delivers small useful steps, shows them early and adjusts. Scrum gives this a rhythm of planning, review and retrospective. | A goal, not just tasks; Working software is progress; Improve every cycle | Plan one week of your own project as a goal and three tasks, then review it. | What is a retrospective for? => Improving how the team works.
tech-writing = Technical writing helps a particular reader do something. Know the reader, say the important thing first and cut the rest. | One idea per sentence; Use active verbs; Test on a real reader | Rewrite a page of instructions at half the length and test it. | What should come first in a document? => What the reader most needs to know or do.
stakeholders = Stakeholders are people affected by the work. Trust grows when they hear news early, in their own terms, with options. | Learn what each cares about; No surprises; Confirm decisions in writing | Map the stakeholders of a project and plan one message for each. | How do you share bad news? => Early, plainly and with options.
estimation = An estimate is a range with assumptions, not a promise. Smaller pieces and past evidence make it better. | Break work down; Give a range; Update when you learn | Estimate three tasks, do them and compare. | Why give a range? => It shows the uncertainty honestly.
portfolio = A portfolio shows how you think. A few finished projects with a clear story beat many unfinished ones. | State problem, choices and result; Link to working code; Say what you would improve | Write a one-page case study of your best project. | How many projects are enough? => Two or three strong ones.
interviews = Interviews test problem solving and communication. Practise aloud, prepare stories with evidence and ask good questions. | Clarify before solving; Explain trade-offs; Prepare five real examples | Record yourself solving one problem and review it. | What if you do not know the answer? => Say so, reason aloud and describe how you would find out.
ux-research = User research replaces opinions with evidence about what people do and need. Match the method to the question. | Watch behaviour, not only words; Avoid leading questions; Small studies reveal big problems | Interview three people about a task and list patterns. | Why not ask "would you use this?" => People predict their own behaviour poorly.
usability-testing = In a usability test someone attempts real tasks while you watch. Their struggles show what to fix. | Give goals, not instructions; Stay quiet; Fix the worst first | Test a site with three people and report the top three problems. | How many people find most major problems? => About five per group is usually enough to learn a lot.
wireframes = A wireframe shows structure without decoration, so people discuss what matters. Prototypes add behaviour so you can test. | Start on paper; Make several options; Match detail to the question | Sketch six versions of one screen in ten minutes. | Why avoid colour at first? => It draws attention away from structure.
design-system = A design system shares decisions: tokens, components and guidance. It saves time and keeps products consistent and accessible. | Tokens name decisions; Document when to use each part; Build access in | Define ten tokens and one documented button. | What makes a system more than a component library? => Shared principles, guidance and ownership.
visual-design = Visual design guides the eye. Size, space, contrast and alignment tell people what matters and what belongs together. | Use fewer sizes and colours; Space groups things; Align to a grid | Improve a cluttered screen using only spacing and type. | What creates hierarchy? => Differences in size, weight, colour and space.
product-metrics = A metric should reflect value to users and change what you do. Vanity metrics grow without meaning much. | Pair a goal with a guardrail; Define it exactly; Look at trends and groups | Choose one main metric and one guardrail for an app. | Why add a guardrail? => It shows harm caused by chasing the main number.
roadmapping = A product roadmap shares direction and priorities. It works best as problems to solve, with less certainty further away. | Now, next and later; Link to goals; Review often | Turn a feature list into a now, next, later roadmap. | Why avoid exact dates far ahead? => Uncertainty is high, so they become false promises.
user-stories = A user story states who needs what and why, with conditions that show it is done. | Keep it small; Write testable criteria; Discuss, do not just hand over | Write one story with three acceptance criteria. | What makes a criterion good? => Anyone can check whether it is met.
app-release = Releasing an app means signing it, passing review, rolling out slowly and watching for crashes. | Protect signing keys; Use staged rollout; Explain permissions | Publish a build to a test track and collect feedback from three people. | Why roll out in stages? => Problems affect few people and can be stopped.
mobile-ux = Phones are used with one hand, in short moments and poor light. Follow the platform so people already know how things work. | Touch targets of at least 44 to 48 points; Keep main actions in reach; Support large text | Review an app one-handed outdoors and list five problems. | Why follow platform conventions? => People already know them, so they learn your app faster.
`;
